WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.076
By the end of this episode, you’ll
have run an automation yourself,

2
00:00:05.076 --> 00:00:08.142
watched it triage a real
task, debugged a failed run,

3
00:00:08.142 --> 00:00:11.092
and approved an outbound
email with your own click.

4
00:00:11.092 --> 00:00:14.910
That’s the whole arc — let’s
start where it ends: the run journal.

5
00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:17.077
Here’s the job.

6
00:00:17.077 --> 00:00:22.428
New tasks land on this board all day, and
someone has to read each one and pick the

7
00:00:22.428 --> 00:00:23.202
right owner.

8
00:00:23.202 --> 00:00:28.230
We’ll hand that to an automation — and
you’ll see exactly how far to trust it.

9
00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.284
First: where automations come from.

10
00:00:32.284 --> 00:00:37.473
The catalog ships ready-made ones —
sync a mailbox, resolve GitHub issues.

11
00:00:37.473 --> 00:00:41.540
One install, and they file
themselves into your workspace.

12
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:46.005
Click a bundle and you
see exactly what you’d get:

13
00:00:46.005 --> 00:00:49.009
the workflows, the
agents, the views it ships.

14
00:00:49.009 --> 00:00:51.229
Nothing runs until you install it.

15
00:00:51.229 --> 00:00:54.820
We can skip it today — the
one we need is already here.

16
00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:57.956
Under Installed: task triage.

17
00:00:57.956 --> 00:01:03.095
It set itself up with this workspace,
and it’s been running quietly the whole

18
00:01:03.095 --> 00:01:03.795
series.

19
00:01:03.795 --> 00:01:07.900
Before we trust it any further,
let’s read what it actually does.

20
00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:14.327
Here’s the workflow behind it — and you
can read it before you ever let it run.

21
00:01:14.327 --> 00:01:16.775
A trigger at the top: every new task.

22
00:01:16.775 --> 00:01:19.951
Then three steps — score
the task with an agent,

23
00:01:19.951 --> 00:01:22.861
route it on the score,
report what happened.

24
00:01:24.061 --> 00:01:28.620
Open the score step and you see
exactly what the agent has to return:

25
00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:32.782
a candidate, a confidence
number, a reason — structured output,

26
00:01:32.782 --> 00:01:33.707
with a schema.

27
00:01:33.707 --> 00:01:35.755
That schema is your safety net.

28
00:01:35.755 --> 00:01:40.380
If the model returns anything else,
the run stops instead of guessing.

29
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.155
Remember that — it comes back in a minute.

30
00:01:44.355 --> 00:01:48.065
The Triggers tab says when
it wakes up: every new task,

31
00:01:48.065 --> 00:01:51.505
and every task someone
moves from Backlog to To do.

32
00:01:51.505 --> 00:01:57.105
The first step filters out tasks that
already have an owner — those need no triage.

33
00:01:58.305 --> 00:02:00.655
You don’t have to wait for a trigger.

34
00:02:00.655 --> 00:02:03.830
Test workflow shows the
exact input a run expects,

35
00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:06.815
and Execute writes a
real run into the journal.

36
00:02:06.815 --> 00:02:11.705
We’ll go one better and trigger it the
way your team will — with a real task.

37
00:02:12.905 --> 00:02:17.017
So — a real task: ‘Prepare
the launch-day social posts’.

38
00:02:17.017 --> 00:02:18.633
Create… and hands off.

39
00:02:18.633 --> 00:02:23.406
A few seconds pass, and the
assignee lands on the card by itself.

40
00:02:23.406 --> 00:02:27.812
The automation read it, scored
it, and picked the Assistant.

41
00:02:27.812 --> 00:02:29.575
Nobody touched anything.

42
00:02:32.905 --> 00:02:35.591
The proof lives with the automation.

43
00:02:35.591 --> 00:02:39.172
Back through the rail:
Automations, task triage,

44
00:02:39.172 --> 00:02:42.902
Executions — and there’s
the new row, seconds old.

45
00:02:42.902 --> 00:02:47.751
Open it and read: what the
trigger saw, the score and its reason,

46
00:02:47.751 --> 00:02:50.064
the route, what each step cost.

47
00:02:50.064 --> 00:02:51.705
Every run leaves this.

48
00:02:52.905 --> 00:02:56.271
Now the row that teaches
you the most: a red one.

49
00:02:56.271 --> 00:02:59.775
Read the journal top to
bottom — the trigger fired,

50
00:02:59.775 --> 00:03:02.592
the score step ran… and there it stopped.

51
00:03:02.592 --> 00:03:06.165
The agent’s answer was
missing the confidence field,

52
00:03:06.165 --> 00:03:11.661
so the schema rejected it, and the run
failed instead of assigning a task on bad

53
00:03:11.661 --> 00:03:12.361
data.

54
00:03:12.361 --> 00:03:14.615
That’s your safety net, doing its job.

55
00:03:15.815 --> 00:03:18.065
And the failure has a fix path.

56
00:03:18.065 --> 00:03:23.073
The journal names the step; the
step holds the prompt and the schema;

57
00:03:23.073 --> 00:03:25.541
Configuration carries the retries.

58
00:03:25.541 --> 00:03:30.185
Journal, step, fix, run again —
that’s the whole debugging loop.

59
00:03:31.385 --> 00:03:34.623
One more boundary
matters: the outside world.

60
00:03:34.623 --> 00:03:39.372
We ask the Assistant to draft a
reply to a customer — and send it.

61
00:03:39.372 --> 00:03:42.826
The draft streams in…
and then everything stops.

62
00:03:42.826 --> 00:03:47.647
Sending mail leaves the workspace,
so that step waits for a person.

63
00:03:47.647 --> 00:03:47.935
You.

64
00:03:57.385 --> 00:03:59.383
Read the draft — it’s good.

65
00:03:59.383 --> 00:04:05.008
We type one note in the box, submit
the response… and only now does the mail

66
00:04:05.008 --> 00:04:06.192
actually go out.

67
00:04:06.192 --> 00:04:08.116
The agent did the writing.

68
00:04:08.116 --> 00:04:09.745
The send stayed yours.

69
00:04:11.385 --> 00:04:14.889
Last stop: the audit log, under Settings.

70
00:04:14.889 --> 00:04:20.358
There’s our approval — who
decided, what, and when: seconds ago.

71
00:04:20.358 --> 00:04:23.776
’, you don’t reconstruct it from memory.

72
00:04:23.776 --> 00:04:26.425
You open this page and read it.

73
00:04:27.625 --> 00:04:28.978
That’s automations.

74
00:04:28.978 --> 00:04:33.322
You read a workflow before
trusting it, triggered a real run,

75
00:04:33.322 --> 00:04:36.954
followed its journal,
traced a failure to its step,

76
00:04:36.954 --> 00:04:38.734
and kept the send button.

77
00:04:38.734 --> 00:04:41.939
The automations and
approvals docs go deeper.

78
00:04:41.939 --> 00:04:46.425
Next episode: projects — your
team and its agents at one board.

79
00:04:47.625 --> 00:04:52.682
Everything you just did is written up
in the documentation, step by step.

80
00:04:52.682 --> 00:04:54.275
See you in episode six.

